<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Our Lady of Guadalupe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1531">1531</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Virgin of Guadalupe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Our Lady of Guadalupe is a title of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a> associated with apparitions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-diego">Juan Diego</a> at Tepeyac, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in December 1531, and with the image venerated in her basilica there. The devotion grew through the colonial period into a symbol shared by indigenous people, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizos</a> and criollos. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a> carried her banner in 1810, and she remains a central emblem of Mexican identity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe.jpg" alt="Our Lady of Guadalupe" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Virgen_de_guadalupe1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church was the central institution of cultural life, and the devotion to the <b>Virgin of Guadalupe</b>, said to have appeared to Juan Diego in 1531, became a shared focus of identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Religious literature includes sermons, plays and the Nican mopohua, the Nahuatl account of the apparitions of the <b>Virgin of Guadalupe</b>, published in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Criollos took pride in their homeland: in 1648 a criollo priest, Miguel Sánchez, published the story of the <b>Virgin of Guadalupe</b>, widely read as a sign of God’s favour to Mexico.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li><li><blockquote>Every year, on the eve of 12 December, Mexico's most famous artists sing mañanitas in honour of <b>Our Lady of Guadalupe</b> at her basilica.</blockquote><a href="/songs/las-mananitas">Las Mañanitas</a></li><li><blockquote>The image of <b>Our Lady of Guadalupe</b> became Mexico's most revered religious symbol.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
